    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d861">

                <pb facs="0912" n="814" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg814"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/1056: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Tenney</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-01-22T16:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 22,
                                1920—4 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received January 24—4:33
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>21. The Japanese Minister presented notification dated January 19th to the
                    Chinese Ministry of Foreign Affairs stating the desire of the Japanese
                    Government to begin direct negotiations through commissioners appointed by the
                    two Governments for the settlement of all questions relating to Shantung.
                    Special mention is made of the organization at an early date of the railway
                    police force, presumably as defined in the exchange of notes following
                    Sino-Japanese loan agreement of September 29th [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >28th</hi>], 1918.<anchor n="58" xml:id="d861-f1"/> The Japanese Minister’s
                    memorandum was confidential but I have been furnished a copy. The Chinese are in
                    a panic realizing that direct negotiations will lead to the strengthening of
                    Japan’s hold on Shantung. They would like to refer the matter to the League of
                    Nations but hesitate to do so unless the United States Government is to be
                    represented in the League of Nations. As soon as the ratification of the treaty
                    by the Senate is accomplished I advise that the United States Government insist
                    upon the reference of the Shantung question to the League of Nations.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Tenney</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d861-f1" n="58" xml:id="d861-f1-content">
                <p>The exchange of notes did not follow the loan agreement; the notes preceded the
                    agreement. For texts of notes, dated Sept. 24, 1918, see <ref
                        target="frus1919v01#pg_571"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>,
                        1919, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, pp. 571</ref>–<ref
                        target="frus1919v01#pg_572">572</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
